course: language
lesson: 38
title: Words that changed their meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Words are not frozen: over hundreds of years their meanings drift, like boats slowly carried along by a river, so an old book can use a word you know in a way that surprises you.
explain: Ella opens a very old book and reads that a man was "silly" because he was happy and blessed; back then "silly" meant blessed, then it drifted to innocent, and only later to foolish.
explain: Some words narrow (they come to mean less: "deer" once meant any wild animal), some widen or pick up new jobs (a "mouse" can now sit next to your keyboard), and some go from good to bad or bad to good.
explain: New meanings often come from technology, jokes, stories and slang, and the old meaning frequently lives on beside the new one.
explain: When something in an old text sounds odd, ask yourself: "Did this word mean something different back then?"
fact: When a word's meaning changes over time, it is called semantic change.
fact: "Silly" comes from Old English "sælig", meaning blessed or happy; it later meant innocent, and then foolish.
fact: In Middle English "girl" meant any young child, boy or girl; later it narrowed to mean only a female child; narrowing is when a word comes to mean something more specific.
fact: Old English "dēor" meant any wild animal, like German "Tier" (animal) today; it narrowed to one kind of animal, the deer, and "hound" once meant any dog, like German "Hund".
fact: Old English "sæd" meant full, as after a big meal; it later came to mean heavy and serious, and then unhappy.
fact: "Naughty" comes from "naught", meaning nothing; it first meant having nothing, then wicked, and now mostly a child behaving badly.
fact: "Starve" comes from Old English "steorfan", to die, like German "sterben"; it narrowed to die of hunger, and "I'm starving" today usually just means very hungry.
fact: Technology gives old words new jobs: a computer mouse, the cloud (online storage), a stream (watching online); the old meanings stay too.
fact: "Spam" for junk messages comes from a 1970 Monty Python comedy sketch in which people sing "Spam" over and over, drowning out everything else; Spam is also a brand of tinned meat.
fact: "Clue" comes from "clew", a ball of thread; in a Greek myth the hero Theseus used a ball of thread to find his way out of a maze called the labyrinth.
fact: In maths and science "random" means by chance; in slang since the 1980s it also means strange or out of place.
fact: In the 1500s "bully" meant darling or sweetheart, probably from a Dutch word; it later meant a fine fellow, then a show-off, and then someone who hurts weaker people.
exercise: Ella reads that a man in an old book was "silly" because he was happy and blessed. What's going on? | "Silly" once meant blessed or happy | The writer made a spelling mistake | The man was really foolish all along | Old books always use words as jokes
exercise: What is it called when a word's meaning changes over time? | Semantic change | Back-formation | A palindrome | A blend
exercise: Theo learns that "girl" once meant what? | Any young child, boy or girl | Only a grown-up woman who was married | A small dog or puppy | A queen or a princess
exercise: What did Old English "dēor" mean? | Any wild animal | Only a deer | A forest | Someone very dear
exercise: Which German word still means animal, like old "dēor"? | Tier | Hamster | Hirsch | Wasser
exercise: When a word's meaning becomes more specific, what is it called? | Narrowing | Widening | Blending | Clipping
exercise: Kai learns "sad" once meant what? | Full, as after a big meal | Angry and shouting at people | Tired and sleepy | Quiet and shy
exercise: What did "naughty" first mean? | Having nothing, being poor | Being cheeky to the teacher at school | Being very clever | Being kind to animals
exercise: Sam reads that "starve" once meant what? | To die | To be a bit hungry | To eat too much | To cook a meal
exercise: Isla's grandma points at an animal and says "mouse"; Isla points at the computer. Who is right? | Both of them | Only Grandma | Only Isla | Neither of them
exercise: Where does "spam" for junk messages come from? | A 1970 Monty Python comedy sketch | A computer company's secret code word for junk | A Latin word for rubbish | A mistake in the first email
exercise: Lucy reads that "clue" comes from "clew". What was a clew? | A ball of thread | A detective's notebook | A small key | A footprint in mud
exercise: Which hero used a ball of thread to escape the labyrinth? | Theseus | Hercules | Robin Hood | Odysseus
exercise: A Gen Alpha boy says "that's so random" about something boring. What does his dad say "random" means? | Chosen by chance | Strange and silly | Very boring | Too loud
exercise: What did "bully" mean in the 1500s? | Darling or sweetheart | A big strong bull | Someone who hurts others | A loud drum
exercise: What does "I'm starving" usually mean today? | I'm very hungry | I'm about to die | I'm completely full up | I'm freezing cold
test: Your mate goes "words never change, bruv, a word means what it means, forever." What's true? | Many words drift in meaning over hundreds of years | He's right, words are fixed when they're first used | Only slang words ever change, never proper ones | Words change only when the government says so
test: Your nan from Bristol says "'ee were a naughty one, my lover." ("My lover" is a friendly greeting in the West Country.) What does "naughty" mean today? | Badly behaved, usually about a child | Having nothing at all, very poor | Wicked and dangerous to everyone around | Blessed and very happy
test: What would happen if you read an old poem where a knight is "silly" and you used today's meaning? | You'd wrongly think he was foolish instead of blessed | You'd understand the poem exactly as the poet meant it | You'd find out the knight was a clown at the court | Nothing, because "silly" has never changed meaning
test: Your American cousin says "y'all, there's a deer in the yard!" ("Y'all" means you all.) In Old English, what could "deer" have been? | Any wild animal at all | Only a deer with antlers | Only a pet dog | Only a bird
test: A Gen Z teen says "my dad's so random, lowkey." ("Lowkey" means a bit.) What does "random" mean here? | Strange and unexpected | Chosen by drawing lots | Very old and wise | Always on time
test: Your bro says "spam was always about emails, that's what the word means." What's true? | It's a tinned meat brand whose name a comedy sketch made into a word for junk | He's right, it was invented by the first email company to name junk messages | It's short for "special mail" from the post office | It's an old Latin word for rubbish
test: In a cartoon a detective says "a clue is a ball of thread, obviously." Could that ever have been right? | Yes, "clue" comes from "clew", a ball of thread | No, a clue has always meant a footprint or fingerprint | No, "clue" was invented by detectives in 1900 | Yes, but only in the Welsh language
test: Your grandad, a big fan of old films, says "Bully for you!" when you win a race. What does he mean? | Well done (an old phrase keeping the good sense of "bully") | You pushed the others like a bully and he's cross with you | You should fight the other runners | You're as strong as a bull
test: Your mate says "a mouse is an animal, the computer one is a mistake." What's the fair answer? | Old words get new jobs with new technology, and both meanings are right | He's right, the computer meaning is just a mistake that people keep making | The computer meaning is older than the animal one | "Mouse" only means the computer thing now
test: Why might comparing an English word with German help you guess its old meaning? | English and German are related, so German sometimes keeps the old sense | German speakers invented every English word and gave them to England later | German words never change meaning at all | English copied all its words from German last century
exam: Identify the term designating the alteration of a word's meaning over the course of time. | Semantic change | Back-formation | Clipping | Inflection
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the history of the word "silly"? | It formerly signified blessed or happy, and only later foolish | It has signified foolish since the very earliest days of Old English | It was borrowed from French in the twentieth century | It formerly signified angry, and only later foolish
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Old English word "dēor". | It denoted any wild animal and subsequently narrowed to a single species | It denoted exclusively the animal now termed a deer, and nothing else whatsoever | It denoted a forest and subsequently an animal | It denoted a domestic dog and subsequently a deer
exam: Ascertain the original meaning of the Old English verb "steorfan", from which "starve" derives. | To die | To be hungry | To eat greedily | To cook
